@page "/autoapi/tutorials"

<TutorialDetail Tutorials="TutorialSet.AutoApi">
<p>Step-by-step guides for using <code>RForge.Blazor.AutoApi</code> in a Blazor Web App. They assume you already know Blazor components, dependency injection, minimal APIs and render modes, and explain only what is specific to the generated API.</p>

<p>Start with <a href="/autoapi/tutorials/01-setup">Setup</a>. After that, each tutorial stands alone: it has its own small example, so you can jump straight to the topic you need.</p>

<div class="table-container">
    <table class="table is-striped">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>#</th><th>Tutorial</th><th>You'll learn</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var tutorial in TutorialSet.AutoApi.All)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@tutorial.Number</td>
                    <td><a href="@tutorial.Url">@tutorial.Title</a></td>
                    <td>@tutorial.Description</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="conventions-used-everywhere">Conventions used everywhere</h2>

<p>Each code block starts with a comment giving the <strong>file path</strong> it belongs in, for example <code>// MyApp.Shared/IBookService.cs</code>. The tutorials use three projects: <code>MyApp.Shared</code> for the interface and its types, <code>MyApp</code> for the server, and <code>MyApp.Client</code> for WebAssembly. That layout is a recommendation, not a requirement. <a href="/autoapi/tutorials/01-setup">Setup</a> lists the few things that are required.</p>

<h2 id="see-also">See also</h2>

<ul>
<li><a href="/autoapi/example/todos">Todos example</a>: a todo list that calls one interface from Interactive Server and from WebAssembly.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/docs/autoapi/spec.md">Specification</a>: every attribute, route and diagnostic in one document.</li>
</ul>
</TutorialDetail>
